  "order": "Deliberate, not alphabetical. `backend/evidence/runner.py` fetches the first six by default, and an alphabetical manifest would make those six all aiohttp -- none of which has a line any rule can act on -- so a default run would read as 0% coverage and look broken rather than honest. The first six span five repositories, six CWEs, and both outcomes: three advisories where a rule engages and three where none does. `python scripts/evidence_report.py --all` runs the remaining thirteen.",
  "caveats": [
    "The vulnerable-file line is found by scanning for a pattern the rule table already matches, so the coverage figure is an UPPER bound on what the engine could find, not a measurement of how many real vulnerabilities it detects.",
    "Excluded from the per-file counts: test, docs, example, benchmark and vendor paths. A fix that rewrites a test is not a fix to the vulnerability.",
    "An advisory fixed by a merge commit is diffed against its first parent, and is marked `is_merge` in the output."
  ]
